  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nNot too many changes with the forecast, patchy river fog each \nmorning continues to be a possibility. Temperatures will remain mild \nfor the forecast period, except excessive cloudiness on Sunday will \nyield a completely average start to November. Dry weather is likely \nfor all the dressed up trick-or-treaters, maybe someone will be \ndressed up as a weather radar this year!\n\nLooking towards the weekend, Halloween itself is likely to be dry \nfor candy hunting, frights and thrills, and costume parties. A \nsubtle shortwave arrives on Sunday, but poor moisture return means \nany rainfall is likely to remain on the lighter side. Guidance has \ncontinued the trend of keeping the rain extremely limited in both \namounts and areal scope, and the NBM has finally caught on to my \nexpectations. High temperatures on Sunday are likely to be more \nmuted thanks to the widespread cloud cover.\n\nNext week we will likely see another couple dry fall weather days, \nleaning on the more mild side similar to this past week. Midweek \ninto late week ensemble guidance continues to depict potential for a \nsouthern US trough that will be the next system to watch to fill our \ngrowing need of rain. The last several runs of both physics based \nand AI guidance indicate potential for mountain wave winds with this \nsystem, so something to watch for both rain and wind potential. \nSince we're still 5 days out, just a watch and see to see if we can \nboth realize these hazards and narrow down the timing for overall \npassage.",
